{"id":46410489282999,"type":"album","name":"The Psychedelic Sounds of the 13th Floor Elevators","artist":"13th Floor Elevators","year":1966,"weight":21,"info":{"cover":"https://dn710206.ca.archive.org/0/items/mbid-5cc205f5-8c8c-332c-8934-3b6346cefe24/mbid-5cc205f5-8c8c-332c-8934-3b6346cefe24-36339656424.jpg","kind":"Album","tracks":27,"country":"XW","yt":"f3gFs648HCQ","desc":"The Psychedelic Sounds of the 13th Floor Elevators is a 1966 album by 13th Floor Elevators. The album's sound, featuring elements of folk, garage rock, blues and psychedelia, is notable for its use of the electric jug, as featured on the band's only hit, \"You're Gonna Miss Me\", which reached number 55 on the Billboard Charts with \"Tried to Hide\" as a B-side. Another single from the album, \"Reverberation (Doubt)\", reached number 129 on the Billboard's Bubbling Under Chart. The November 1966 album title is purported to be the first use of the word \"psychedelic\" in reference to the music .","links":{"rateyourmusic":"https://rateyourmusic.com/release/album/the-13th-floor-elevators/the-psychedelic-sounds-of-the-13th-floor-elevators"}},"artist_ref":{"id":142511372769830,"name":"The 13th Floor Elevators","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_13th_Floor_Elevators_%281960s_Photo%29.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[{"id":147423806094006,"type":"genre","name":"Psychedelic Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":690,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":207419651019004,"type":"genre","name":"Garage Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":556,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"},{"id":166279679595706,"type":"genre","name":"Acid Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":248,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":15604072550254,"type":"genre","name":"Garage Psych","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":201,"yt":null,"src":"rym"},{"id":22716476580322,"type":"genre","name":"Texas Psychedelia","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":54,"yt":null,"src":"rym"}],"lists":[{"id":8722801824226